<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple Computer//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  WHY THIS EXISTS. Without it, every SecTrustStoreSetTrustSettings() call
  returns -34018 (errSecMissingEntitlement) on real hardware. Measured, not
  predicted: the first real run reported

      cainjector: 0001.der: SecTrustStoreSetTrustSettings -> -34018
      cainjector: 0001.der: NOT present after install (Contains -> 0)

  121 times over. That same log also settled a real open question in this
  package's favour: the hand-declared prototypes in SecTrustStorePrivate.h are
  CORRECT. A wrong ABI does not produce a specific, documented errSec code and
  a clean errSecSuccess from SecTrustStoreContains alongside it; it produces a
  crash or nonsense. Reads need no entitlement, writes do.

  THE NAME IS NOT GUESSED. It is a literal in this firmware's own
  /usr/libexec/securityd (Security-57317.40.2), right beside the file that
  implements the check and the database it guards:

      modify-anchor-certificates
      /Library/Keychains/TrustStore.sqlite3
      /SourceCache/securityd/Security-57317.40.2/Security/sec/securityd/SecTrustStoreServer.c

  HOW AN AD-HOC SIGNATURE CARRIES IT AT ALL. It does not, on a stock device --
  that is the entire point of an entitlement. It works here because the
  untether patches AMFI/cs_enforcement, which is what would otherwise refuse a
  binary claiming an entitlement it was never granted. This is the same
  property that lets unsigned bash and sshd run at all, applied to a signature
  blob rather than to execution. If a future firmware or a different
  persistence path leaves AMFI intact, this stops working and cainjector will
  say so plainly in its own log rather than silently installing nothing.

  IF THIS ROUTE FAILS, DO NOT WRITE THE DATABASE DIRECTLY. securityd's own
  strings do name the backing store (/Library/Keychains/TrustStore.sqlite3),
  and this tool does run as root on a device with a patched kernel, so it
  would be physically possible -- and it is ruled out by decision, not by
  difficulty. Going through Apple's own API means securityd owns the schema,
  the locking and the cache invalidation; hand-writing that database means
  this project owns all three forever, against a format with no documentation
  and no stability guarantee, on the one device state where getting it wrong
  breaks TLS for everything rather than for one repo. If the entitlement stops
  working, find out why it stopped working.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>modify-anchor-certificates</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
